WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.280
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.730
It's the inventor of the World Wide Web,

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.203
Sir Tim Berners-Lee.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.900
When it comes to computer science,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.310
it doesn't get much bigger
than inventing the web.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.600
Not many developments
have had such an impact

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.770
on the way we live

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.630
and arguably the way we think and behave.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.170
His former boss's initial
description of his idea

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.470
as 'vague but exciting',

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.050
might seem modest now
but that's an indication

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.600
of the unforeseeable change it created.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.700
Tim Berners-Lee has devoted his time

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.493
to advocating web freedom,

00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:37.290
and access through his
World Wide Web Foundation.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.130
Now he wants to challenge the power

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.100
of big organizations online

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.340
and reassert the web's
original principles.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.623
But is it too late?

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.130
Here's a taste of what's coming up.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.180
How happy are you with the digital world?

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.200
Recent events and including the pandemic

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.078
has heightened people's
understanding out there

00:00:57.078 --> 00:00:59.531
that this is not just,

00:00:59.531 --> 00:01:02.750
it is a right but it's not something

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.583
you can take for granted,

00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:04.690
you have to fight for it.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.010
When I've seen you,

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.950
when I've been actually
face-to-face with you,

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:09.790
with your creation

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.801
and there's only one that
really comes to mind,

00:01:11.801 --> 00:01:13.250
which was your cameo

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.990
in the London 2012 Olympic
Games Opening Ceremony.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:21.410
You were on stage in an amazing
looking sort of lit up house

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.130
and you look like you're typing,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.500
and then a message appeared
across the audience,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.157
beamed across the audience which said,

00:01:27.157 --> 00:01:29.140
"This is for everyone."

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.023
How did that feel doing that?

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:32.710
And you know being on that showcase.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.289
It was beeping amazing.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.750
Sir Tim, welcome to the program.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:51.990
It's lovely to have you here

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.820
and we're gonna come to your
latest project very soon,

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.120
but I wanted to ask first of all,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.890
is it a burden being Tim Berners-Lee,

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.820
the man who invented the World Wide Web?

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.870
I wouldn't say it's a burden.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.708
It doesn't seem from my
point of view to be a burden,

00:02:06.708 --> 00:02:11.189
in that I'm not that famous
for people recognizing me

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:12.703
in the supermarket.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.280
On the other hand inventing
the World Wide Web

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.501
means you do get invited

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:21.313
to all kinds of great dinners
and meet all kinds of people.

00:02:21.313 --> 00:02:23.730
So my life gets more interesting,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.563
just for that,

00:02:24.563 --> 00:02:25.764
so I'm not a burden.

00:02:25.764 --> 00:02:27.410
And I wonder for you,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.830
is there a pressure with it sometimes?

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.663
'Cause when, I don't know,

00:02:30.663 --> 00:02:32.070
if I was sat next to you at a dinner

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.910
and I asked you what you do?

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:34.860
And you say,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.210
of course what you're working on now,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.470
oh I happen to invent this thing called

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:39.420
the World Wide Web.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.310
Hm-mm.
Do you feel a pressure

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.180
then to talk about
things you've done since,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.160
or how that compares?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.917
I've learned from bitter experience that

00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:48.730
it's a good idea to say early on,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.310
by the way I invented
the web 30 years ago.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.202
Just about that small thing
Get it on the table.

00:02:52.202 --> 00:02:55.442
rather than spring it after
five hours of conversation.

00:02:55.442 --> 00:02:58.390
So that,

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.300
but I think there's certainly,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.440
you know when we started
the Web Foundation

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.940
there was a feeling that you
can't just invent something

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.556
when it gets to the 20,

00:03:08.556 --> 00:03:11.204
the point that 20% of
the world is using it,

00:03:11.204 --> 00:03:14.400
then there is a responsibility.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.370
And so it wasn't just me

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.250
but all the people involved in
making the web more powerful,

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.780
realized that every step they
made the web more powerful,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.294
the gap in power between those who had it

00:03:24.294 --> 00:03:26.580
and those who hadn't was increased,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.400
so the digital divide was increased

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.810
every time we made the web better.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.900
And so that meant we had a,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.882
the Web Foundation was founded basically

00:03:34.882 --> 00:03:39.217
partly on simply if 80%
of the web at the time,

00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:41.640
of the world wasn't on the web,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:42.990
then we should,

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.000
the other 20% has a duty.
Yeah.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.180
To get them on work,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.850
to get them on,

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.230
now it's more than half
the world is on the web.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.063
So it's saying,

00:03:52.063 --> 00:03:53.730
we've got lots of momentum
in that direction but,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.590
yes I think there's a
responsibility both for getting

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:57.850
the other people on,

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.310
but also then particularly
in more recent years

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:04.130
realizing that it's not obvious
that the web will actually

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.614
be a hundred percent a good thing.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.405
And actually what you also
have to constantly be looking

00:04:10.405 --> 00:04:11.640
for what could go wrong

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.220
and what has gone wrong

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.610
and what could be fixed.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.530
How happy are you with the digital world?

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.695
Stepping back very much in general,

00:04:19.695 --> 00:04:22.020
I think you can't complain

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.140
because, and I think the pandemic,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.610
I think has shown a lot of people

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.300
who didn't really think
about it beforehand,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.170
that actually you know,

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:31.820
if they have the internet
they can work from home,

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:32.900
if they don't have the internet

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:33.733
then they can't

00:04:33.733 --> 00:04:34.566
work from home.
Hm-mm.

00:04:34.566 --> 00:04:36.480
And then maybe people also think

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.689
a little bit more about the fact that

00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:41.680
there hasn't been very much
censorship in the country

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.630
where they live,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.910
but they look and they see
that people in other countries

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.360
have the internet blocked
completely during elections,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:48.470
for example, and so on.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.330
So I think that recent events,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.610
including the pandemic have heightened

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.225
people's understanding out there

00:04:55.225 --> 00:04:57.700
that this is not just,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.120
it's the internet is a right,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.690
but it's not something
you can take for granted

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:02.960
you have to fight for it.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.310
Yes, and the fight for
the rights of information,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.350
but also the correct information,

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:08.410
free information,

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.600
not fake news.
Hm-mm.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.570
And not have that layer on it.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:12.930
How do you think we're doing with that?

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.980
'Cause some would say the
internet has made things better

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:16.990
in many ways,

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.961
but worse on some of those fronts.

00:05:18.961 --> 00:05:20.622
I think it really depends on

00:05:20.622 --> 00:05:22.520
which system you're talking about.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.347
So different social media systems

00:05:24.347 --> 00:05:26.060
are designed to work in different ways.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:27.750
You could like things
and the like buttons,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:28.802
and the poke,

00:05:28.802 --> 00:05:30.171
sort of the workflow you go through

00:05:30.171 --> 00:05:34.630
to encourage each other and
give feedback to each other

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:35.480
is different.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.120
And it turns out they have
very different effects.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.199
So it turns out that early on

00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:42.610
feminist bloggers found
that being on Twitter

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.330
was just horrible, for example.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.941
And, so other than,

00:05:46.941 --> 00:05:49.952
the questions about being on Instagram,

00:05:49.952 --> 00:05:53.140
if you're a teenage girl it can be,

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.180
it can have psychological issues

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.712
just with the things that
the platform seems to do,

00:05:57.712 --> 00:05:59.600
suggest that you should do.
Hm-mm.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.450
That you have to be infinitely beautiful

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:02.283
and things like that.

00:06:02.283 --> 00:06:04.010
So these are all different platforms.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.400
So it's not the web as a whole,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.710
it's a question of

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:07.720
the design.
Of what's been built

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.600
on top of it?
Of the platforms, yeah.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.693
So what I tell people is look,

00:06:10.693 --> 00:06:12.959
you know, to start with,

00:06:12.959 --> 00:06:14.160
if you're a coder,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.811
whether or not you're a coder,

00:06:15.811 --> 00:06:18.490
if you can imagine a social
network that works differently

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:19.400
and you think it would work

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.540
and you can imagine it would work.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.820
It would be a better place to be.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.910
Or maybe you can imagine
a social network which,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:28.430
where people would tend
to be more constructive,

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.700
where you tend to get more
truth out of it than untruth

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.050
because people be more
collaborative and more,

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:34.883
it'd be,

00:06:34.883 --> 00:06:36.033
have a better culture then,

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:38.260
then design that social network.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.640
But do you-
Or go to the people.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.030
But that sounds like you believe

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.530
design could be the answer,

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:44.920
and that it can be a
better world can be baked

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.331
into the design rather than

00:06:46.331 --> 00:06:48.010
what some people say which is,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:52.440
'These platforms reflects back
at us, the societal ills.'

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.739
So it's not the platform's fault,

00:06:53.739 --> 00:06:55.099
it's who we are.

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:58.300
And therefore they don't
think the technology companies

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.170
should do more for instance,

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.918
to get rid of the toxicity
that many women find online.

00:07:02.918 --> 00:07:04.230
I think that's,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.850
it's naive to imagine in a way

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:09.510
it's simplistic to imagine
that things like Twitter

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:10.598
and Facebook and Instagram,

00:07:10.598 --> 00:07:13.022
and so on, and Reddit.

00:07:13.022 --> 00:07:15.160
That they are simple mirrors.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.486
Initially, I think when the early,

00:07:17.486 --> 00:07:18.640
maybe we sort of,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.720
in the pre-web days when
people were using internet

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.000
to exchange files,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.630
the meme was that the internet is neutral.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.370
The humanity out there

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.680
is good and bad.
Yes.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.120
When you connect to people you know,

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.830
you could join them in an
IRC chat room back there

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:32.820
before the web,

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.379
and you would find good and bad people.

00:07:34.379 --> 00:07:38.019
But actually now because we
have these different platforms

00:07:38.019 --> 00:07:39.000
and because they're different,

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:39.833
actually no,

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.630
the Web Foundation in
fact did some studies

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.720
and looked at when they were
looking at misogyny online,

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.030
they were looking at gender rights

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:54.000
and online gender violence.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.830
And they found for example,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.390
three things where a social
media company could actually

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:00.879
change the way the system worked

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:03.396
and it would make it better for women.

00:08:03.396 --> 00:08:06.290
And they went to those different companies

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:09.117
and they suggested it
and the changes were made

00:08:09.117 --> 00:08:10.920
and the web got better.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.920
So there is a huge response.
I'd like to feel

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.788
some of those changes
that you're talking about.

00:08:15.788 --> 00:08:16.950
Maybe it yes,

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.690
maybe they didn't fall a
huge apples right next to you

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:23.490
but the messages really that the way you

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.190
design it is important.
Yeah.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:26.844
Humanity is important too.

00:08:26.844 --> 00:08:28.167
So when we do,

00:08:28.167 --> 00:08:29.980
everybody needs to help.

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:33.200
So governments need to help by

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.160
getting the laws in place,

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.183
things that are really, really nasty,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:38.603
and illegal,

00:08:38.603 --> 00:08:40.220
that are illegal.
Yeah.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:41.550
People need to,

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.420
before they retweet something,

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:44.580
before they like something,

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.460
they need to also learn to
actually do a double take.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.170
But would you agree that it's gone,

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.500
it's going the other way?

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.780
'Cause I always envisioned,

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:52.890
and I used to work

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.170
as a technology correspondent.
Hm-mm.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.740
That our offline sales
and our online sales

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:57.930
would come closer,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.770
as we got more used to
using these platforms,

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.458
we would try and not do something online

00:09:02.458 --> 00:09:04.479
that we wouldn't do in real life.

00:09:04.479 --> 00:09:05.940
And yet we seem to have done this.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.700
We project an image of who we want to be.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.700
And because of people
being able to be anonymous,

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.150
they are doing different
things than they would do

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.890
if they saw you in the street.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.840
So what you're talking about when you say

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.937
when you talk about
people being anonymomys

00:09:19.937 --> 00:09:21.819
and maybe you're talking about Twitter.

00:09:21.819 --> 00:09:22.930
Maybe you're not,

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:24.380
you're not talking about.
Or in the,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.810
in the comments below articles

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:27.680
or on videos,

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:28.513
on this video you know,

00:09:28.513 --> 00:09:29.870
people can say whatever they want

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.540
but they wouldn't say it to your face.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.570
I think basically stepping back

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.420
each of these, the things which go wrong

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.810
with each of these spaces is depends

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:43.729
on the way they're designed

00:09:43.729 --> 00:09:46.271
that and they,

00:09:46.271 --> 00:09:48.860
and in each case the issues are different.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.731
In each case you need
to do lots of research

00:09:50.731 --> 00:09:53.590
to understand exactly how the badness

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:54.813
and the goodness propagate.

00:09:54.813 --> 00:09:57.920
So you can maybe amplify
the goodness a bit.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:59.623
So yeah,

00:10:00.603 --> 00:10:05.230
I still believe that
being responsible online

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:06.980
is part of it.
Yeah.

00:10:09.435 --> 00:10:12.000
You mentioned the World
Wide Web Consortium

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.130
of which you're a Director,

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.520
the plan and the goal is obviously to hold

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.450
Big Tech companies to account,

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.070
but there has been concern
because of how it's funded

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:20.960
with Big Tech,

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:22.000
helping to fund it

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.510
that there is a conflict of interest

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.189
and I suppose taking a step back,

00:10:26.189 --> 00:10:28.930
there is a concern about who marks

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:30.880
these few tech giants homeworks',

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.040
who does hold them to account.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.570
When we have so many governments

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.240
who aren't tech savvy at all.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:36.838
